// Plush guestbook client
//
//   plush guestbook.psh [--local | --localhost | server_address]
//
// Connects to the guestbook at plushlang.org by default, to a server on
// this machine with --local or --localhost, or to the given address, e.g.
// example.com:1337.
//
// Connects to a Plush guestbook server and shows the latest messages, which
// anyone can read. Signing the guestbook needs an account: sending a message
// pops up a box to register or log in, then posts it. The message list
// scrolls with the scrollbar on the right or the up and down arrow keys,
// and older messages load as you scroll down. Admins can press F12 to see
// server information, then F1 to return to the guestbook.
//
// F2 shows the pixel canvas, a large image that anyone can draw on without
// an account: pick a color, then click pixels to paint them. The arrow keys
// move around the canvas, and + and - zoom in and out. F1 returns to the
// guestbook.
//
// F3 shows the circuit, a large grid that anyone can build logic circuits
// on without an account: pick a node, turn it with R, then click cells to
// place it. Clicking a switch turns it on or off, and the right mouse
// button empties a cell, as does the eraser, which D picks. The circuit is
// simulated here, see Sim, so what its flip-flops hold differs from one
// client to the next.
//
// The server speaks one JSON object per line in each direction. Passwords
// are never sent: the client sends a SHA-256 hash of the password instead.
// The canvas and the circuit are fetched as tiles, each a TGA file sent as
// raw bytes after the response that announces it.

from image import Image, rgb, RAINBOW12, decode_tga;
from json import parse_json, format_json;
from sha256 import sha256;
from ./monogram_font import Font;

